{% set local_problem %}
<p>We've worked on enough Hartselle homes to know that downspouts are often overlooked. Older homes downtown frequently have original downspouts that are undersized by modern standards. Newer construction often uses builder-grade downspouts that meet minimum code but can't handle heavy storms.</p>

<p>Hartselle's mix of town properties and more rural acreage creates varied drainage challenges. In-town lots have specific grading patterns. Properties outside town often have different soil compositions and drainage needs related to the agricultural land use throughout Morgan County.</p>

<p>When downspouts can't handle the volume, gutters back up and overflow. When downspouts dump water too close to the foundation, that water pools and causes damage. Both problems are common — and both are fixable.</p>

<p>As your hometown company, we understand Hartselle's specific drainage patterns. We'll assess your property and recommend exactly what you need — no more, no less.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three essential components: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and effective water dispersal.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized appropriately for your roof area and our area's rainfall. We often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 inch — they handle heavy storms without backing up.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally flows to corners and low points in your gutter system.</p>

<p>Water dispersal means getting that water far from your foundation. Basic splash blocks move water a few inches. Extensions move it a few feet. For properties with challenging drainage, underground drains route water 10+ feet away.</p>
{% endset %}

